To rigorously assess the crashworthiness of a structure, several key performance indicators are derived from the force–displacement response obtained during quasi-static compression testing. The Total Energy Absorption (EA), which reflects the energy dissipated throughout the crushing process, is quantified as the area under the force–displacement curve and calculated by the integral: \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym}
				\usepackage{amsfonts}
				\usepackage{amssymb}
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$EA=\underset{0}{\overset{ \delta }{\int }}F\left(x\right)dx$$\end{document} The terms F(x) and δ denote the instantaneous crushing force and the crushing distance, respectively. Another essential parameter is the Specific Energy Absorption (SEA), which normalizes the absorbed energy by the mass  m  of the specimen, enabling comparison across different materials and geometries: \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym}
				\usepackage{amsfonts}
				\usepackage{amssymb}
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$SEA=EA/m$$\end{document} The Mean Crushing Force (MCF), defined as the average force sustained over the displacement range, is computed by the following formula. \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym}
				\usepackage{amsfonts}
				\usepackage{amssymb}
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$MCF=EA/\updelta$$\end{document} MCF provides insight into the structure’s load-bearing behavior during the entire deformation process; a higher MCF typically indicates superior energy absorption capacity. To evaluate the efficiency of energy absorption, the Crush Force Efficiency (CFE) is introduced. It is defined as the ratio of the mean force to the Peak Crushing Force (PCF) the maximum force recorded during initial yielding: \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym}
				\usepackage{amsfonts}
				\usepackage{amssymb}
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$CFE=MCF/PCF$$\end{document} CFE values range between 0 and 1, with values approaching 1 denoting optimal crash performance, characterized by a nearly rectangular force–displacement curve with a long, stable plateau [ 47 ]. The initial stiffness (k) of the structure, representing its resistance to elastic deformation, is calculated from the slope of the force–displacement curve in the linear elastic region [ 48 ].
